1
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,490
 Seeking fortune in America.

2
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,700
 You heard about Christopher Columbus, truly.

3
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:52,690
 And Cortez in Mexico.

4
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:03,160
 Pizarro, who went to Peru.

5
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:11,920
 Go back a little in time and take another look.

6
00:02:13,150 --> 00:02:17,030
 I said we'll be going back in time.

7
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:18,170
 Let's go. There we are.

8
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:19,650
 We're in 1513.

9
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,700
 Let me tell you a bit about Nunez de Balboa,

10
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,380
 who after a troublesome voyage caused by a false maneuver

11
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:29,420
 on the part of his second-in-command, Pizarro,

12
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,240
 somehow found the Pacific Ocean

13
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:33,440
 and solemnly claimed possession of it

14
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:35,480
 in the name of their Catholic majesties.

15
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:41,820
 But the short-tempered governor, Pedrarius, had a score to settle with Balboa, who, as it happens, is his son-in-law.

16
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:48,240
 With help from that same Pizarro, he has him decapitated, and in those days, you might hear anything said of the Indies.

17
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:53,840
 Some said that there lived blue men with square heads, and other men who had only one leg.

18
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:57,400
 Some swore there were unicorns, dwarfs, giants.

19
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:06,010
 Some sailors swore they saw a golden man, mermaids.

20
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:15,270
 It's not true. How could anybody really believe that?

21
00:03:15,270 --> 00:03:16,890
 But they did believe it, and fervently.

22
00:03:17,130 --> 00:03:20,610
 Look here, some were sure on Bimini Island there was a fountain of youth.

23
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:23,840
 Yes, an old person could bathe in it and be young again.

24
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:25,760
 I'd be willing to give it a try.

25
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,590
 That's right, a fountain that would restore youth.

26
00:03:32,220 --> 00:03:35,900
 Once a de Leon, who was no spring chicken at the time.

27
00:03:36,340 --> 00:03:37,820
 Imagine, he was 53.

28
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:42,280
 Well, he believed there was a fountain of youth, and he even became governor of Bimini for that.

29
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:47,040
 He was an old soldier who had been companion to Columbus and to Cortez.

30
00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:51,490
 You see it, Lopez? I saw some branches moving over there.

31
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:53,880
 Indians, perhaps? Take your men and have a look.

32
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:54,990
 Yes, Commander.

33
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:21,890
 Think the water, of course.

34
00:04:44,470 --> 00:04:51,700
 On Easter Day, 1513, all these flowers, really a pretty sight.

35
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:55,990
 Oh, in the name of our sovereign king, I take possession of this land, Pasqua, Florida.

36
00:04:57,530 --> 00:05:01,550
 Yes, flowering Easter, and hence the name Florida.

37
00:05:02,890 --> 00:05:08,370
 Once at De Leon, would explore this land, sometimes at great inconvenience to himself.

38
00:05:09,150 --> 00:05:12,390
 During one of his explorations, he discovered the Gulf Stream.

39
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:17,870
 And, of course, the land that would later be called Cape Canaveral.

40
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:29,940
 He must have been the first European to set foot on the future USA.

41
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:41,230
 In Florida, Ponce de Leon will be seriously wounded.

42
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:44,870
 Oh yes, he'll die in Cuba a few days later.

43
00:05:44,870 --> 00:05:54,010
 Some years later, Panfilo de Narvaez, the one who had been sent to fight Cortez, is named governor.

44
00:05:54,010 --> 00:06:01,120
 From Florida to Mexico. Of course, it still remained to conquer the region.

45
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:07,530
 Pabessa de Vaca, cow's head. I must say, it is certainly a very curious name.

46
00:06:07,530 --> 00:06:13,450
 Sir, I am very proud of my name. My forefathers earned it by charging the enemy with bullhorns having no weapons.

47
00:06:13,670 --> 00:06:19,290
 I am well aware you are all valiant, brave soldiers here. I've decided to take you with me to Florida.

48
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:24,930
 I am the new governor. You will be in command of a ship, and you will also be treasurer of the expedition.

49
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:26,380
 So, will you accept?

50
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:27,460
 Mm-hmm.

51
00:06:28,230 --> 00:06:28,890
 Sign there.

52
00:06:29,030 --> 00:06:30,510
 I agree, Governor Narvaez.

53
00:06:30,510 --> 00:06:51,630
 Hunter Domingo was selected as the first port of call.

54
00:06:54,560 --> 00:07:00,160
 But with all the disorder and uncertainty of the expedition, 150 men see their chance and jump ship.

55
00:07:03,030 --> 00:07:06,000
 To Trinidad for our provisions.

56
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:07,060
 Is this weather, sir?

57
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:10,050
 You just follow orders, right?

58
00:07:16,190 --> 00:07:31,730
 That's how the expedition lost two vessels and 160 men.

59
00:07:33,950 --> 00:07:35,550
 We can leave for Florida.

60
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:39,290
 Pretty good idea how to get there.

61
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:39,450
 No!

62
00:07:40,290 --> 00:07:43,260
 We found a navigator who knows the region, Miruelo.

63
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:54,170
 Youth, yes?

64
00:07:54,820 --> 00:07:56,260
 Did you ever find it?

65
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,270
 Not exactly. We got lost.

66
00:07:58,350 --> 00:07:59,080
 Bad luck.

67
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:01,880
 Here is starboard course.

68
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,620
 That's the port side, Marlboro, not starboard.

69
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:06,760
 Yes, of course. That's what I meant.

70
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:16,230
 We headed for shallow water?

71
00:08:17,450 --> 00:08:21,650
 There's no shallow around here. Nothing to worry about here.

72
00:08:22,970 --> 00:08:24,370
 Sail with your eyes shut.

73
00:08:26,850 --> 00:08:30,090
 Strange going on. Looks like deep water's shallower around here.

74
00:08:30,330 --> 00:08:34,490
 Don't worry about it. We'll be up.

75
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:37,120
 It needs a little more water.

76
00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:46,340
 Instead of laying on this happenin' sea dog,

77
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,930
 this is just one of those things you learn to live here.

78
00:08:48,930 --> 00:08:54,420
 Now, ships remained stuck for two weeks until another storm

79
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:58,360
 set them floating again, just as everyone had given up hope in the expedition.

80
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:05,200
 And suddenly, they reached their objective, Tampa Bay in Florida, the very spot where Ponce de Leon had been mortally wounded.

81
00:09:07,450 --> 00:09:12,910
 Say, there are many like this one up in the country they call Appalachia.

82
00:09:12,970 --> 00:09:15,230
 Yes, Appalachia, that way, that way, you go.

83
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:20,440
 The decision is made. We will go on foot, the ships will sail up the coast and join us later.

84
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:23,240
 There's a sheltered port that Miruelo knows.

85
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:27,160
 Look here, Navarez, consider, can you really trust a man like him?

86
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:30,060
 We'd best not leave our ships or we're lost.

87
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:32,610
 Assemble the men on deck now, that's an order.

88
00:09:33,830 --> 00:09:39,380
 The nation is mad! All of you are going to die!

89
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:40,580
 They're waiting you!

90
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,580
 Quite possibly some among us are bound to die,

91
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:46,580
 but for the others, there will be wealth, glory, and fame and power!

92
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:50,140
 Our husbands are going off, risking their lives, not caring about us.

93
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:53,140
 All right, when they have gone, we will forget them.

94
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:55,140
 We will find new husbands, right?

95
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:56,140
 Right!

96
00:09:56,140 --> 00:10:42,370
 And off they went, 300 men, mostly to die.

97
00:10:42,370 --> 00:10:44,500
 It's the last morsel. Now we have no more food.

98
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,920
 This can't be far now. The ships will be waiting for us.

99
00:10:47,920 --> 00:10:50,920
 Well, in a pinch, we could cook up our horses.

100
00:10:50,920 --> 00:11:09,480
 Strange men, they will die to get yellow metal.

101
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:24,260
 This is it, Appalachee, we're here.

102
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:34,040
 No, don't do that! Stop!

103
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:49,070
 No ghosts! All these men are no more than peasants!

104
00:11:49,070 --> 00:11:51,070
 But you were wrong to attack them!

105
00:11:51,070 --> 00:11:52,590
 It's not over yet. Look!

106
00:11:52,590 --> 00:12:04,680
 The level of the father, the salt of the holy ghost.

107
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:10,620
 The ships still aren't here.

108
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:13,610
 If we stay here any longer, we'll starve to death.

109
00:12:13,610 --> 00:12:16,050
 Build us some boats.

110
00:12:16,050 --> 00:12:18,050
 We will sail back to Mexico.

111
00:12:18,050 --> 00:12:27,060
 A violent storm. Narva has disappeared. Forever.

112
00:12:27,060 --> 00:13:04,460
 The men. Him all.

113
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:06,750
 And you will save us as well.

114
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:28,200
 ...their newly acquired profession as healers.

115
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:31,120
 And, my golly, they had some success.

116
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:33,140
 But they were hoping for other things.

117
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:34,820
 And together with their new friends,

118
00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:38,830
 they cross into Alabama and then into Mississippi and Louisiana,

119
00:13:39,150 --> 00:13:41,940
 all the way to Texas, across the Rio Grande.

120
00:13:41,940 --> 00:13:45,650
 And then they're back in Mexico, over 6,000 kilometers,

121
00:13:45,870 --> 00:13:47,170
 and they did it in eight years.

122
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:51,560
 Slave!

123
00:13:53,210 --> 00:13:58,090
 We're Christians. I'm a Spanish officer. Take me to your commander.

124
00:14:05,710 --> 00:14:09,030
 De Vaca was governor and then deprived of his title and put in prison.

125
00:14:09,030 --> 00:14:15,020
 You know why? Just because he was too friendly with the Indians, whom he had learned to respect.

126
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:16,980
 Such is life.

127
00:14:21,870 --> 00:14:33,550
 Again, at Tampa Bay in Florida, run into an old acquaintance of ours, Hernando de Soto.

128
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:40,810
 This brilliant horseman had performed a remarkable circus trip for the Inca, Atahualpa.

129
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:50,660
 And in time, he is appointed governor, and he, too, one day sets off looking for the cities of gold that Cabeza de Vaca had heard of.

130
00:14:51,110 --> 00:14:56,710
 And he takes a real army with him, 600 men, over 200 horses, and some Indians.

131
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:00,230
 Look, Luis, Indians announcing our arrival.

132
00:15:01,460 --> 00:15:03,100
 He has smoke signals everywhere.

133
00:15:13,390 --> 00:15:18,600
 He'd be good to eat?

134
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:19,280
 I don't know.

135
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:20,160
 Try him.

136
00:15:20,160 --> 00:16:09,670
 I was a soldier in the expedition that was led by Narais.

137
00:16:09,890 --> 00:16:11,950
 I have been a prisoner of the Indians for 12 years.

138
00:16:12,150 --> 00:16:14,950
 The Indians, are they as numerous and as rich as they say?

139
00:16:15,150 --> 00:16:17,540
 Oh, they are poor. Very poor.

140
00:16:18,380 --> 00:16:20,920
 Numerous? More than the trees in this forest.

141
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:24,020
 But they can hide in the forest like the very leaves on the trees.

142
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:27,500
 And all the same, it's said that up north there are villages of riches.

143
00:16:27,640 --> 00:16:28,960
 I don't know where they are.

144
00:16:29,430 --> 00:16:34,100
 You come along. We'll feed you and put clothes on your back worthy of a Spaniard.

145
00:16:34,890 --> 00:16:39,370
 Venice sent you. You will be our interpreter with the Indians.

146
00:16:46,350 --> 00:16:53,540
 He says, welcome to all of us coming to visit, and we are invited to spend the night in his village.

147
00:16:55,850 --> 00:17:00,420
 Wouldn't you agree? It is very nice of him to receive us so kindly, Captain.

148
00:17:00,660 --> 00:17:02,620
 Look at the splendid food they have provided.

149
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:06,330
 Have you noticed? Almost none of the men are here. I don't like this.

150
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:15,420
 Never mind, it's a trap!

151
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:57,360
 Soto, 40 hours like that.

152
00:17:57,860 --> 00:17:59,820
 Yeah, they were strapping fellows back then.

153
00:18:00,050 --> 00:18:01,440
 With plenty of courage.

154
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:14,620
 These losses and for nothing.

155
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:17,810
 Indians have deserted. We'll need slaves to replace them.

156
00:18:20,790 --> 00:18:26,070
 Listen, Hernando, all we have found here is misery and death for many.

157
00:18:26,070 --> 00:18:28,770
 Don't you think it's better to give up here, return home?

158
00:18:28,970 --> 00:18:31,670
 Our boats are waiting at Pensacola. It's six days March.

159
00:18:31,870 --> 00:18:37,190
 No, no, Luis. Remember Peru. It may be here, very near, or a little to the north or west.

160
00:18:37,310 --> 00:18:38,290
 Divaca said so.

161
00:18:44,030 --> 00:18:51,360
 Sometimes I don't understand these Indians.

162
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:55,880
 They can be peaceful and give us all they have, and then they will attack us for no reason.

163
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:56,960
 No, it's quite simple, really.

164
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:00,860
 I mean, they will attack us if we try to steal from them or take them as slaves.

165
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:02,340
 It's a logical point of view.

166
00:19:02,830 --> 00:19:05,870
 But you don't create an empire with that sort of logic.

167
00:19:05,970 --> 00:19:25,950
 You don't conquer gold.

168
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:28,670
 They could furnish some.

169
00:19:28,830 --> 00:19:31,170
 No, tell them that we need 200 porters.

170
00:19:33,210 --> 00:19:45,490
 take up positions just in case we need porters 200 we present you gift isn't much changed our

171
00:19:45,490 --> 00:20:09,400
 friend de soto old ways die hard especially for a conquistador their bloody battle pretty spanish

172
00:20:09,400 --> 00:21:14,900
 soldiers would lose their lives and ten times more indians we have found only death and misery in

173
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:31,870
 this land of suffering yes we shall go home it's the only way four long years this nightmare journey

174
00:21:31,870 --> 00:21:39,930
 went on chasing an illusion and over half the men already perished my dear friends my faithful

175
00:21:39,930 --> 00:21:45,290
 companions i admit i was wrong throughout this long adventure i never stopped dreaming of peru

176
00:21:45,290 --> 00:22:03,200
 and there is no peru here it's all louise will take you back to our people forgive me and another

177
00:22:03,200 --> 00:22:09,540
 brave man died pursuing a mad dream but it's not over yet viceroy mendoza is impatient waiting for

178
00:22:09,540 --> 00:22:15,140
 the brave de soto who himself is stuck in alabama while waiting he raises an even bigger army a

179
00:22:15,140 --> 00:22:23,800
 1,000 infantry soldiers, hundreds of cavalry, Indians, and it's the dashing young governor, Francisco Velasquez de Coronado, who will be in command.

180
00:22:24,340 --> 00:22:28,960
 And this time, they will leave from Mexico and move up the Pacific coast.

181
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:40,560
 These are ones, the famous seven golden cities of Cibola.

182
00:22:41,820 --> 00:22:44,260
 Have you any gold?

183
00:22:44,380 --> 00:22:52,030
 In you if you have yellow metal, chick.

184
00:22:52,030 --> 00:22:56,550
 Listen, a key river. Father, there is gold, much gold.

185
00:22:56,730 --> 00:22:59,510
 And there are rivers where there are fish, big fish, goldfish.

186
00:23:00,070 --> 00:23:23,760
 Great love, very big cugas on the prow, our eagles, golds, yes, golds!

187
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:45,080
 Best of their Eldorado, their city of gold, coming and going, wandering like lost souls.

188
00:23:53,670 --> 00:24:03,870
 Tell him if he knows where yellow metal is any gold. Ask him how we can get to Kiriva.

189
00:24:04,160 --> 00:24:07,840
 Kiriva is one day's march that way. I will take you there if you want me to.

190
00:24:08,260 --> 00:24:10,900
 Tell him we accept. He will lead us to Kiriva.

191
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:17,640
 Ask him if he knows yellow metal and where to find some.

192
00:24:19,360 --> 00:24:19,880
 Gold.

193
00:24:20,420 --> 00:24:26,560
 He says he knows where on it, just like his necklace.

194
00:24:27,180 --> 00:24:28,920
 That's copper, not gold.

195
00:24:29,540 --> 00:24:33,130
 Say, you, you're the one who said there was plenty of gold around here.

196
00:24:33,190 --> 00:24:35,250
 You know what happens to traitors?

197
00:24:39,310 --> 00:24:45,160
 Another total failure.

198
00:24:45,320 --> 00:24:48,220
 The era of the great conquerors is now drawing to a close.

199
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:55,020
 Oh, there will still be Cabrillo, Unamo, Viscaino, Onate, Marendes, and others,

200
00:24:55,160 --> 00:25:00,240
 but never again would anyone witness the miraculous success of Cortes and Pizarro,

201
00:25:00,550 --> 00:25:04,630
 before whose armies enemy forces parted, as the Red Sea before Moses.

202
00:25:04,770 --> 00:25:08,670
 But all the same, it certainly seems that a strange curse followed our conquistadores.

203
00:25:09,120 --> 00:25:13,320
 Remember the sad fate awaiting Christopher Columbus, the discoverer of America,

204
00:25:13,460 --> 00:25:15,920
 who was already abandoned by everybody.

205
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:19,150
 The end is if I'd been given more time.

206
00:25:19,150 --> 00:25:24,390
 The miserable end of Cortes, weary of this world,

207
00:25:24,490 --> 00:25:28,650
 Alvarado, his seething second-in-command, off a precipice on horseback.

208
00:25:29,100 --> 00:25:30,280
 Pizarro and his brothers.

209
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:33,060
 Francisco, his throat cut by Almagro's son.

210
00:25:33,580 --> 00:25:36,900
 Juan, his younger brother, stoned to death by the Indians.

211
00:25:37,100 --> 00:25:39,380
 Gonzalo, decapitated for rebelling.

212
00:25:40,620 --> 00:25:42,890
 Almagro, strangled in his cell.

213
00:25:43,960 --> 00:25:47,260
 Diego, his son, executed in the same place as his father.

214
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:49,640
 Balboa, falsely accused of treason.

215
00:25:50,320 --> 00:25:52,460
 Ponce de Leon died from his wounds.

216
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:56,180
 Narvaez, lost at sea.

217
00:25:56,180 --> 00:26:03,130
 De Soto, the valiant, worn out by fever, and cast into the Mississippi River, and all the others.

218
00:26:03,350 --> 00:26:10,210
 No, even if fate brought them glory, destiny was not kind to these men.

219
00:26:10,890 --> 00:26:17,570
 These conquistadores, with all their faults and excesses, made theirs an exceptional century, the conquest of a new world.

